        "summary": "This book introduces act (energeia) and potency (dynamis)—the most important distinction for understanding motion, change, and the structure of created being.\n\nDefinitions:\n• **Potency**: The capacity or ability to be otherwise, to undergo change or to have something done to it. A acorn has the potency to become an oak; bronze has the potency to become a statue.\n• **Act**: The actualization or realization of that capacity. An oak tree is an acorn actualized; a completed statue is bronze actualized.\n\nFundamental Principle:\nActuality is prior to potentiality in three ways: in time (what is actual causes potentiality to be realized), in essence (the fully realized thing is what potentiality aims at), and in causality (the actualizing agent is always prior).\n\nApplication to Change:\nChange is the actualization of potential—a thing moves from potentiality to actuality. Understanding this resolves ancient puzzles: the Eleatics wrongly thought change impossible because they saw no way for being to come from non-being, but potentiality is a real principle distinct from both pure being and non-being.\n\nThe Divine Intellect:\nGod is pure act (actus purus) without potentiality—eternally thinking, unchanging, perfect. Creatures, by contrast, always contain potentiality; they are always capable of becoming, changing, being otherwise.\n\nSignificance:\nAct/potency is the master-key to comprehending all reality from God to inanimate matter. It explains motion, explains why things are finite and mutable (they contain potentiality), and points to the necessity of a pure act at the summit of being."
