        "summary": "The Metaphysics culminates in theology—the study of God or the divine principle. Aristotle argues that there must exist an eternal, unchanging, immaterial substance—the unmoved mover—whose very existence is the source of all motion and being in the cosmos.\n\nThe Argument:\nMotion is a fact. But motion requires a cause; an infinite regress of movers is impossible. Therefore, there must be an unmoved mover—something that causes motion without itself moving, whose eternal actuality draws all things toward it like a beloved draws a lover.\n\nNature of the Unmoved Mover:\n• **Eternal and Unchanging**: It exists necessarily and cannot be otherwise.\n• **Immaterial and Incorporeal**: It is pure form without matter, hence immune from potentiality, change, and decay.\n• **Pure Act (Actus Purus)**: It is not composed of potentiality and act like creatures; it is actuality alone.\n• **Self-Thinking Intellect**: Its activity is thinking, and its object is itself—eternally knowing itself in an act of supreme intellection.\n• **Separate Substance**: It exists apart from the material, changing world, yet moves that world by being the object of desire and love.\n\nCosmological Order:\nThe unmoved mover is surrounded by concentric spheres (reflecting ancient astronomy)—the heavens with their eternal, circular motions. Each sphere is moved by a separate unmoved mover, arranged in a hierarchy of intellects, all ultimately grounded in the first.\n\nDivine Perfection:\nGod's perfection consists in eternal self-thinking: \"Thought thinks upon itself, taking as its object that which is most divine and precious; and its thinking is a thinking upon thinking.\" God's life is the most blessed—infinite intellection without need, without striving, without the imperfection of becoming.\n\nInfluence on Creation:\nThe divine intellect eternally contains the forms of all things—God knows all essences in their infinite perfection. Creation flows eternally from God's necessity (not from will or desire), as light flows from the sun.\n\nCulmination:\nMetaphysics reaches its height in theology: the recognition that there exists a necessary, eternal, perfect being—the source of all contingent being, the ground of all motion, the object of all desire. In knowing this, the human intellect participates in the activity proper to the divine intellect and achieves the highest wisdom."
