        "summary": "This book examines mathematical objects—numbers, lines, surfaces, solids—and their relationship to sensible substances. Are mathematical objects separate from sensible things, or do they exist only in sensible particulars? This inquiry critiques Platonism and clarifies what mathematics studies.\n\nThe Platonic View Examined:\nPlatonists claim that mathematical objects (numbers, geometric forms) exist as separate, eternal substances. Aristotle argues this view is incoherent:\n• If numbers were separate substances, they could not explain the sensible world (from which they are separate).\n• Universal objects (like \"three-ness\" or \"triangle-ness\") cannot be individual substances; universals are predicated of many, while substances are singular.\n• If mathematical objects were separate, they would need their own causes and principles—yet we observe that mathematics has no independent science of generation or corruption.\n\nAristotle's Position:\nMathematical objects exist in sensible particulars, apprehended by intellect through a process of abstraction. A geometer studying a bronze sphere considers only its shape and size, ignoring the bronze itself. The line exists as the boundary of a body; the point, as the limit of a line. Mathematical objects are real, but their being depends on sensible substances; they are not self-subsisting entities.\n\nThe Reality of Mathematics:\nYet mathematics is not subjective or merely mental. Mathematical truths are necessary and eternal because they reflect the intelligible structure inherent in sensible things. The mind truly grasps this structure when it abstracts, even though the abstracted object has no existence apart from matter.\n\nConclusion:\nMathematics is the science of quantity as an intelligible form in sensible bodies, not of separate eternal substances. This preserves the reality and necessity of mathematics while rejecting Platonism's multiplication of mysterious entities beyond the sensible world."
