        "summary": "The Metaphysics concludes by synthesizing its entire inquiry. Being and unity are convertible transcendentals—whatever exists is one, and vice versa. This final book examines their relationship and the ultimate principles underlying all reality.\n\nBeing and Unity:\nBeing and unity are not genera with a common definition, but analogues—they are related as diverse expressions of the same underlying actuality. To be is to be one; to be multiple is already to be many unities.\n\nThe Principle of Unity:\nUnity in creatures always involves composition: matter/form, essence/existence, substance/accident. Only God is absolutely simple—pure unity, pure actuality, without any composition or potentiality.\n\nPrinciples of Being:\nMetaphysics identifies several irreducible principles:\n• **Form and Matter**: Their union constitutes sensible substance.\n• **Act and Potency**: Their relationship explains motion, causation, and the hierarchy of beings.\n• **Substance and Accident**: Substance is primary; accidents depend on it.\n• **The One and the Many**: Unity and multiplicity are complementary aspects of being, not contradictory.\n\nThe Problem of Predication:\nHow can one universal nature (humanity) exist in many particulars (individual humans)? Aristotle's solution: the nature exists potentially in many as a universal; it is actualized in each particular. Unity and multiplicity cohere through the act/potency distinction.\n\nCosmic Order:\nAll beings are arranged in a graduated hierarchy, from prime matter (pure potentiality) through living things to eternal, immaterial substances (God and the separate intelligences). Each level actualizes the potentiality of the level below it.\n\nFinal Wisdom:\nThe highest wisdom is knowledge of the divine—the eternal, perfect, self-thinking intellect. In contemplating this, the human soul achieves its highest perfection, participating in the divine life. This is the goal of all philosophy: to know the highest things and their first causes, to understand why all things are as they are, and to recognize the One from which all being flows."
